{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '170,280p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go; rg -n 'func (discoverValidationModules|moduleValidationChecks|materialWorktreeClean|materialTrackedUnchanged)|func .*ValidationModule' pose-mcp/internal/cli/*.go; sed -n '1040,1155p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; rg -n 'carry|selfContained' POSE.md | head -15; rg -n 'carry|unchanged' .pose/knowledge --glob '*.md' | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"// with an expiring execution identity before the Harness may run it. The\n// local boundary never executes isolation-required checks.\n\ntype executionPlan struct {\n\tSchemaVersion int           `json:\"schema_version\"`\n\tGeneratedAt   string        `json:\"generated_at\"`\n\tProjectID     string        `json:\"project_id\"`\n\tSpec          string        `json:\"spec,omitempty\"`\n\tGitHead       string        `json:\"git_head,omitempty\"`\n\tMatrixSHA256  string        `json:\"matrix_sha256\"`\n\tChecks        []checkResult `json:\"checks\"` // isolation-required only\n\tApproval      planApproval  `json:\"approval\"`\n}\n\ntype planApproval struct {\n\tRequired bool   `json:\"required\"`\n\tIdentity string `json:\"identity,omitempty\"` // expiring execution identity (ADR-007), stamped externally\n\tExpires  string `json:\"expires,omitempty\"`\n}\n\nfunc writeExecutionPlan(path string, plan executionPlan) error {\n\tpayload, err := json.MarshalIndent(plan, \"\", \"  \")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn os.WriteFile(path, append(payload, '\\n'), 0o644)\n}\n\nfunc writeValidationJSON(path string, run validationRunResult) error {\n\tpayload, err := marshalValidationJSON(run)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn os.WriteFile(path, payload, 0o644)\n}\n\nfunc marshalValidationJSON(run validationRunResult) ([]byte, error) {\n\tpayload, err := json.MarshalIndent(run, \"\", \"  \")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn append(payload, '\\n'), nil\n}\n\n// configuredDeliveryResultsPath identifies the project-local JSON artifact\n// that downstream delivery and closeout gates consume. Other --json-out\n// paths remain ordinary exports and keep their existing writer behavior.\nfunc configuredDeliveryResultsPath(root string) string {\n\tpolicy, err := posemodel.LoadDeliveryPolicy(root)\n\tif err != nil || policy.ResultsPath == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn normalizeValidationOutputPath(policy.ResultsPath)\n}\n\nfunc normalizeValidationOutputPath(path string) string {\n\tif path == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(filepath.FromSlash(path)))\n}\n\n// publishCanonicalValidationJSON rechecks the delivery policy after acquiring\n// the root lease, then publishes the exact canonical result through B14. A\n// changed path is refused before creating or replacing the result.\nfunc publishCanonicalValidationJSON(root, relative, expectedPolicyPath string, run validationRunResult) error {\n\treturn publishCanonicalValidationJSONIn(cliGovernedStore(root), relative, expectedPolicyPath, run)\n}\n\nfunc publishCanonicalValidationJSONIn(store posemodel.Store, relative, expectedPolicyPath string, run validationRunResult) error {\n\treturn store.WithGovernanceWriteGuardForProjects(\"validate.publish-results\", nil, func(store posemodel.Store) error {\n\t\tpolicy, err := posemodel.LoadDeliveryPolicyIn(store.FS, store.Root)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\tcurrentPath := normalizeValidationOutputPath(policy.ResultsPath)\n\t\tif currentPath == \"\" || currentPath != expectedPolicyPath || currentPath != normalizeValidationOutputPath(relative) {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"configured delivery results path changed before publication; nothing was written\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tpayload, err := marshalValidationJSON(run)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\ttarget := filepath.Join(store.Root, filepath.FromSlash(currentPath))\n\t\tif err := store.FS.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(target), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn store.FS.Publish(target, payload, 0o644, posemodel.PublishAuthoritative, posemodel.PublishExpectation{Mode: posemodel.ExpectAny})\n\t})\n}\n\n// --- JUnit projection -------------------------------------------------------\n// Mapping: one <testsuite> per module, one <testcase> per check. POSE\n// \"error\" maps to <error> (infrastructure), \"fail\" to <failure>, \"skipped\"\n// to <skipped> with the deterministic reason. Optional-severity failures are\n// still <failure> — JUnit has no tolerated level, so the POSE severity is\n// preserved in the testcase classname suffix (documented lossy edge).\n\ntype junitTestCase struct {\n\tXMLName   xml.Name      `xml:\"testcase\"`\n\tName      string        `xml:\"name,attr\"`\n\tClassName string        `xml:\"classname,attr\"`\n\tTime      string        `xml:\"time,attr\"`\n\tFailure   *junitMessage `xml:\"failure,omitempty\"`\n\tError     *junitMessage `xml:\"error,omitempty\"`\n\tSkipped   *junitMessage `xml:\"skipped,omitempty\"`\n}\n\ntype junitMessage struct {\n\tMessage string `xml:\"message,attr\"`\n\tBody    string `xml:\",chardata\"`\npose-mcp/internal/cli/discovery_submodule_test.go:75:func TestDiscoverValidationModules_RespectsGitignoreInsideSubmodule(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/stack_manifest_test.go:80:func TestDiscoverValidationModulesDetectsCloudflareWorkers(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:667:func discoverValidationModulesIn(files posemodel.Files, root string) ([]validationModule, error) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:734:func discoverValidationModules(root string) ([]validationModule, error) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1571:func moduleValidationChecks(module validationModule, matrix validationMatrix, stackFilter, moduleFilter string) (string, []validationCheck, bool) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1696:func materialWorktreeClean(root string) bool {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1729:func materialTrackedUnchanged(root string) bool {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_root_and_nodemodules_test.go:78:func TestDiscoverValidationModules_IgnoresQwenWorktrees(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_root_and_nodemodules_test.go:109:func TestDiscoverValidationModules_IgnoresFixtureDirectories(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_root_and_nodemodules_test.go:141:func TestDiscoverValidationModules_RespectsGitignore(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_root_and_nodemodules_test.go:176:func TestDiscoverValidationModules_ClassifiesAndroidSeparatelyFromJava(t *testing.T) {\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(matrixPath)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: validation matrix not found at %s\\n\", \"Erro: matriz de validação não encontrada em %s\\n\"), matrixPath)\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tmatrix, err := parseValidationMatrix(raw)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: invalid validation matrix: %v\\n\", \"Erro: matriz de validação inválida: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif err := validateStructuredMatrixPaths(matrix); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: invalid validation matrix path: %v\\n\", \"Erro: path inválido na matriz de validação: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif err := validateDeliveryMatrixContract(matrix); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: invalid delivery validation contract: %v\\n\", \"Erro: contrato de validação de delivery inválido: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif matrixHasLegacyChecks(matrix) {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintln(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: legacy shell 'command' checks are unsupported; migrate each check to program + args + env.\", \"Erro: checks shell legados em 'command' não são suportados; migre cada check para program + args + env.\"))\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif mode == \"\" {\n\t\tmode = matrix.Defaults.Mode\n\t\tif mode == \"\" {\n\t\t\tmode = \"strict\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tmodules, err := discoverValidationModules(root)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: discovering modules: %v\\n\", \"Erro: descobrir módulos: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\treturn 1\n\t}\n\tif workspaceFilter != \"\" {\n\t\tresolved, ok := resolveWorkspaceModulePath(root, modules, workspaceFilter)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: no module's manifest name matches --workspace %q.\\n\", \"Erro: nenhum module tem name de manifesto igual a --workspace %q.\\n\"), workspaceFilter)\n\t\t\treturn 2\n\t\t}\n\t\tmoduleFilter = resolved\n\t}\n\tknownModules := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, module := range modules {\n\t\tknownModules[module.Rel] = true\n\t}\n\tfor rel, override := range matrix.ModuleOverrides {\n\t\tclean := filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(rel))\n\t\tif clean == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(clean, \"../\") || filepath.IsAbs(rel) {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: moduleOverrides contains a path outside the project: %s\\n\", \"Erro: moduleOverrides contém path fora do projeto: %s\\n\"), rel)\n\t\t\treturn 2\n\t\t}\n\t\tif knownModules[clean] {\n\t\t\tif override.Stack != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tfor idx := range modules {\n\t\t\t\t\tif modules[idx].Rel == clean {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tmodules[idx].Stack = override.Stack\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tabs := filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(clean))\n\t\tif info, err := os.Stat(abs); err == nil && info.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tstack := override.Stack\n\t\t\tif stack == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tstack = \"contract\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tmodules = append(modules, validationModule{Rel: clean, Abs: abs, Stack: stack})\n\t\t\tknownModules[clean] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(modules, func(i, j int) bool { return modules[i].Rel < modules[j].Rel })\n\tvar scope *scopeSelection\n\tif changedTo != \"\" && changedFrom == \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintln(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: --changed-to requires --changed-from.\", \"Erro: --changed-to exige --changed-from.\"))\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif changedFrom != \"\" {\n\t\tsel, err := computeChangedScope(root, changedFrom, changedTo, modules)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: changed-scope selection: %v\\n\", \"Erro: seleção por escopo alterado: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\t\treturn 2\n\t\t}\n\t\tscope = &sel\n\t\tif explain {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"[changed-scope] %s: %d changed file(s), %d/%d module(s) selected\\n\", sel.rangeLabel(), len(sel.Changed), len(sel.Selected), len(modules))\n\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"  + %s: %s\\n\", m.Rel, reason)\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"  - %s: not affected by %s\\n\", m.Rel, sel.rangeLabel())\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\trun := validationRunResult{\n\t\tSchemaVersion: validationResultSchema,\n\t\tGeneratedAt:   time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339),\n\t\tMode:          mode,\n\t\tStackFilter:   stackFilter,\n\t\tModuleFilter:  moduleFilter,\n\t\tChecks:        []checkResult{},\n\t}\n\tmatrixDigest := sha256.Sum256(raw)\n\trun.MatrixSHA256 = \"sha256:\" + hex.EncodeToString(matrixDigest[:])\n\trun.EngineVersion = validationEngineIdentity()\n\t// The head is pinned before anything is planned: selection, carried\n\t// results and the end-of-run comparison all name this one commit (review\n\t// round 5, N14).\n\tif head, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 1024, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"); err == nil {\n\t\trun.GitHead = strings.TrimSpace(string(head))\n\t}\n\tstartedClean := materialWorktreeClean(root)\n\tvar carryState carryPlan\n\tif carry {\n\t\tcarryState = planCarry(root, canonicalResultsPath, mode, run.MatrixSHA256, run.GitHead, matrix, modules)\n218:              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev] | --carry] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\n353:  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). Changed names are read NUL-delimited and a rename names both its source and its destination. Each changed file selects its most specific owning module, every module beneath it (a gitlink or replaced directory changes what lies under it), and every module whose declared `dependsOn` contains it, lies beneath it or is the root `.`, even after that dependency is deleted. A file no module owns selects the root module. Module metadata that does not decode stops the selection (a full run under `--carry`, an error under `--changed-from`). A change outside every module runs every module that does not declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata (uncertainty prefers safe execution; the declaration states that the module's checks read only its own directory and its `dependsOn` modules); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\n355:  **Carried checks:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` refreshes the canonical result instead of rerunning the whole matrix. It uses the current canonical result as its base when the base is comparable: same schema, mode, matrix digest and `engine_version` (the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from; a build of a modified tree never carries), unfiltered, not cancelled, recorded `material_clean` (it validated an unchanged working copy of its own head, and HEAD did not move while it ran) with no unavailable material binding, with its `git_head` an ancestor of `HEAD`, a clean material working copy now, no module or declared dependency reached through a symlink, and no module sharing its directory with another module. The head is pinned before planning, and a run whose HEAD moved records its material bindings as unavailable. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select between the base head and `HEAD`; the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings ignore (data files that are still regular files in the derived-record directories under `.pose/`: indexes, results, state, assessments and review records; the validation matrix stays material) are not changes. A module whose checks are not all in the base also runs. Every other check keeps the base result, its outcome included, and records `carried_from` (the head it ran at, kept through chained carries) and `carried_generated_at`; the delivery index reads that head for the result, so evidence freshness judges the commit a carried check really observed. A carried failure still fails the run. Any unmet condition runs everything, and `carry.reason` says why. `carry` records the base, the number of changed files and each selected module's reason, and `counts.carried` the reused checks. `--carry` does not combine with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n371:- `review-plan` / `review-check` / `closeout-check` — resolve a deterministic component-aware plan from governed metadata, typed overlays and a closed native-tool catalog, then validate immutable attempts against both scope and plan digests. Opt in explicitly with review-policy schema v2, `component_aware: true` and `component_aware_adopted_at`; preview the migration read-only with `pose review-plan <scope> --explain` before committing the policy. Completed attempts older than that component adoption date remain auditable, while open scopes require the new plan. `pose review record` is dry-run by default, accepts `--plan-digest` to reject drift and appends with `--apply`; recommended tools are never executed implicitly. Hierarchical closeout rolls approvals through `spec:`, `milestone:` and `roadmap:` scopes. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_plan`, `pose_closeout_state`. The JSON of `closeout-check` and `pose_closeout_state` types its blockers in `diagnostics` (code, domain, refs, satisfaction condition; text blockers the engine cannot type yet are carried as `opaque`) and its next action in `next_step`; readiness `waiting_on` entries carry a `code`, and auto-attest pendencies a `code`, `source` and `condition` (spec `pose-typed-producer-diagnostics`). The prose fields stay for people; consumers read the codes.\n380:- `artifact-check` — parses exact `### Artifacts` actions, resolves an explicit base/head range or `POSE-Spec: <slug>` commit trailers with safe structured Git arguments, and reports resolvability, existence, action mismatch, undeclared and orphan findings. A claim on a changelog fragment that `pose release prepare` archived resolves through the release manifest, which records the fragment's spec and digest; prepare never edits a spec. A file a later spec removes needs no longer exist for the earlier specs that declare it `created` or `modified`: their claim is satisfied — an edge `removed-by` in `delivery-integrity.json` — when the later spec declares `removed:` for the path, its attributed change sets contain a commit that deleted it (a rename is not a deletion), and every change the earlier spec made to the path — a merge, a root creation, a type change or a deletion included — is a Git ancestor of that commit; a change after the removal, a removal its spec never performed, a change set naming something other than an immutable commit, or history Git cannot read — a shallow clone included; replace refs and grafts are ignored — keeps the existence finding, and the finding says why. Commits modifying artifacts declared by a spec must carry a `POSE-Spec: <slug>` trailer in their commit message for `artifact-check` and `pose close` to attribute the changes. `pose report --change-from/--change-to` persists immutable change-set evidence; `pose index` projects claims, observations, reverse provenance and findings into `delivery-integrity.json`. The seven files `pose index` writes are one generation (spec `pose-index-generation-integrity`): their bytes are written under `generations/<id>/` in the indexes directory, the legacy names are published from the same bytes, and the descriptor `generation.json` there — SHA-256 of each file plus the digests of the inputs they were computed from — is published last and is the commit. A reader fixes the generation the descriptor names and checks each file against it: a set that does not verify (a file missing, replaced, from another generation, or a descriptor it cannot read) is `unavailable`, with the reason, never an empty or mixed result; a repository that never ran `pose index` since this contract keeps working and is reported `legacy-unversioned`, with no claim of coherence. A generated index edited by hand has no effect while the generation's copy exists (readers read the generation), and in a clone without the copy it makes the set unavailable; either way `pose index --verify` names the file. `pose index --verify` reads without writing and exits 1 unless the set is current, the legacy names are those of the generation and its inputs (specs, roadmaps, policy, module metadata, validation matrix) still hold. A failed publication leaves the previous generation in force and a retry converges on the same identity; publishers hold a generation lock in the indexes directory (it never waits: a second `pose index` is refused with `index-generation-busy` and writes nothing, and a lock whose holder is proven dead is recovered), the first publication of a repository is marked pending so one that did not commit reads as `unavailable` and not as a mixture of two runs, an operation that reads several indexes (the review plan) fixes one generation for all of them, and a commit whose durability could not be confirmed is reported as committed; retention keeps the current generation and the newest others up to three, never anything younger than ten minutes, and a retention failure is a warning, not a failed publication. Limits: Git history is read by the delivery graph but not pinned by the descriptor, and a clone without the git-ignored `generations/` directory verifies the legacy names against the descriptor. MCP: `pose_delivery_integrity`.\n385:- `state --attention` (specs `pose-obligation-projection`, `pose-state-attention`) — live, read-only answer to \"what is still owed, by whom, restricting which phase?\". Obligations are projected from the subsystems that own them — readiness (unmet `depends_on`, Definition of Ready, unexplained `blocked`), closeout and review of in-progress specs (an unapproved review, each judgment criterion a sealed bundle still owes, text blockers carried as `legacy-opaque`), start reconciliation when atomic start is adopted, and open follow-ups as advisory residual debt — never stored a second time. Project-level sources are projected too (spec `pose-attention-projects-every-source`), originating on `xref:<project>/project:<project>`: an open docs review mark (owed by the doc's owner until `pose docs-review resolve`), a capability mechanism with a stale trigger, a release newer than the newest verified one that is not yet verified, and an accepted-risk or follow-up finding past its review date in the latest legacy review record. Free-form investigation notes carry no state and are not a source. The render shows coverage first: a producer that failed, or action requests until adopted, makes the answer `INCOMPLETE`, so an empty group never reads as nothing owed, and a fresh install, once committed, reads `complete`. Then what needs `--actor` (or any person or role), then what restricts each phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`), then residual debt. `--json` returns the report — stable `obl-` ids, qualified refs, reason codes, conditions, recipients, effects, satisfaction, knowledge, waiting, a snapshot of revision/dirty tree/policy/contract/index digests, per-producer coverage — plus the `attention` grouping. MCP: `pose_obligations` returns the same ids from the same function. Attention is not a gate.\n650:names the matrix did not carry is reported as one `validation-check` fact per\n660:trailers also name other specs cannot be split by commit; its facts carry\n739:two directories are the contract — policy and profiles carry their own schema\n760:`agent:`/`human:` prefixes remain labels only. The attestation must carry a\n778:`closeout-check` print one assurance line and carry an `assurance` object in\n792:claim names that human. Records written before this carry no block and render\n821:An answer proof authorizes its answer only. New waiver, cancellation and supersession events carry `lifecycle_version: 1`. Under verified action policy they require an operation-bound SSHSIG (`pose-action-lifecycle`) or a trusted issuer's schema-2 action claim. MCP always requires this proof. Both supplied emitters must verify; human presence follows the current explicit channel scope.\n909:`pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\n948:Reservations live in `.pose/review-attempts/`, final receipts link the run, and `.pose/review-run-ledger/` witnesses exact run bytes and transcript digests. Every publication uses the exclusive synced publisher. New runs carry `ledger_version: 1`, `attempt_id` and the optional reason; legacy schema-1 runs retain their original identities, with no invented reservation or signature. An incomplete reservation, missing witness/run/transcript, inconsistent receipt, unknown record or exhausted read budget is explicitly incomplete. Inspect it; no automatic deletion, truncation, repair or retry occurs. Upgrade writers sharing these directories together. Run continuity and authenticated provenance remain separate deliveries.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:32:when the spec closes, and retired when the ADR and `docs-site/docs/cli.md` carry\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:113:Three persistence paths exist, and they carry different things — worth keeping\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:419:- `pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go` — findings already carry\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md:44:deliberately left unchanged (Decision 2 in the spec above): narrowing the\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:45:separate mechanism carrying separate self-referential data (JSON policy\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-28-handoff-delegated-conclusions-round4.md:28:The Unix backend propagates flock errors unchanged. A synthetic overlay returning syscall.EOPNOTSUPP, with only the wait bound reduced from two minutes to 200 ms, reached the deadline in 201 ms and reported another conclusion holding the lock. It published zero proofs and attestations. This is injected error-path evidence, not a claim that the local filesystem lacks flock. Evidence: .review-work/round4/permanent.log, permanent-overlay.json, permanent_lock.go and permanent_wait.go (local, untracked).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1571,1655p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; sed -n '1690,1770p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; sed -n '667,737p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; sed -n '1100,1145p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; cat .pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1571,1655p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; sed -n '1690,1770p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; sed -n '667,737p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; sed -n '1100,1145p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go; cat .pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md\"","aggregated_output":"func moduleValidationChecks(module validationModule, matrix validationMatrix, stackFilter, moduleFilter string) (string, []validationCheck, bool) {\n\toverride := matrix.ModuleOverrides[module.Rel]\n\tstack := module.Stack\n\tif override.Stack != \"\" {\n\t\tstack = override.Stack\n\t}\n\tif stackFilter != \"\" && stack != stackFilter || moduleFilter != \"\" && module.Rel != moduleFilter {\n\t\treturn stack, nil, false\n\t}\n\tchecks := append([]validationCheck(nil), matrix.Stacks[stack].Checks...)\n\tif override.ReplaceDefaultChecks {\n\t\tchecks = nil\n\t}\n\treturn stack, append(checks, override.Checks...), true\n}\n\n// tailLines keeps the last n lines of a check's captured output: enough to see\n// why it failed, with the full output one path away.\nfunc tailLines(text string, n int) string {\n\tlines := strings.Split(strings.TrimRight(text, \"\\n\"), \"\\n\")\n\tif len(lines) <= n {\n\t\treturn strings.Join(lines, \"\\n\")\n\t}\n\treturn strings.Join(lines[len(lines)-n:], \"\\n\")\n}\n\nfunc validationSummaryOf(run validationRunResult) *validationSummary {\n\tsummary := &validationSummary{Outcome: run.Outcome}\n\tfor _, check := range run.Checks {\n\t\tcommand := strings.TrimSpace(check.Program + \" \" + strings.Join(check.Args, \" \"))\n\t\tif check.Outcome != \"skipped\" && command != \"\" {\n\t\t\tsummary.Commands = append(summary.Commands, command)\n\t\t}\n\t\tline := \"- [\" + check.Outcome + \"] \" + check.ID\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase check.SkipReason != \"\":\n\t\t\tline += \" (\" + check.SkipReason + \")\"\n\t\tcase check.DurationSeconds > 0:\n\t\t\tline += fmt.Sprintf(\" (%.1fs)\", check.DurationSeconds)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif check.ExitCode != nil {\n\t\t\tline += fmt.Sprintf(\" exit=%d\", *check.ExitCode)\n\t\t}\n\t\tsummary.Results = append(summary.Results, line)\n\t}\n\t// The report records the run's verdict too, built from the outcome rather\n\t// than copied from the printed line, so the recorded evidence keeps its\n\t// shape while the terminal is free to change.\n\tif run.Counts.OptionalFailed > 0 {\n\t\tsummary.Results = append(summary.Results, fmt.Sprintf(\"Warning: %d optional check(s) failed.\", run.Counts.OptionalFailed))\n\t}\n\tswitch run.Outcome {\n\tcase \"fail\":\n\t\tsummary.Results = append(summary.Results, \"Result: FAILURE (required check failed)\")\n\tdefault:\n\t\tsummary.Results = append(summary.Results, \"Result: SUCCESS\")\n\t}\n\treturn summary\n}\n\nfunc sanitizeGoCheckArgs(ctx context.Context, dir, program string, args []string) []string {\n\tif program != \"go\" || len(args) == 0 {\n\t\treturn args\n\t}\n\n\thasWildcard := false\n\tfor _, a := range args {\n\t\tif a == \"./...\" || strings.HasSuffix(a, \"/...\") {\n\t\t\thasWildcard = true\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !hasWildcard {\n\t\treturn args\n\t}\n\n\thasNodeModules := false\n\t_ = filepath.WalkDir(dir, func(p string, d os.DirEntry, err error) error {\n\t\tif err == nil && d.IsDir() && d.Name() == \"node_modules\" {\n\t\t\thasNodeModules = true\n\t\t\treturn filepath.SkipDir\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil\n\t})\n\tif !hasNodeModules {\n\treturn newArgs\n}\n\n// materialWorktreeClean reports whether the working copy equals HEAD in every\n// path outside the administrative classes, untracked files included. A status\n// that cannot be read is not clean.\nfunc materialWorktreeClean(root string) bool {\n\tout, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 16*1024*1024, \"status\", \"--porcelain=v1\", \"-z\", \"--ignore-submodules=none\", \"--untracked-files=all\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\trecords := strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\")\n\tvar administrative []string\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(records); i++ {\n\t\trecord := records[i]\n\t\tif len(record) < 4 {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpaths := []string{record[3:]}\n\t\tif record[0] == 'R' || record[0] == 'C' || record[1] == 'R' || record[1] == 'C' {\n\t\t\t// A rename or copy carries its source as the next record.\n\t\t\tif i+1 < len(records) {\n\t\t\t\ti++\n\t\t\t\tpaths = append(paths, records[i])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\t\tif !posemodel.IsAdministrativePath(p) {\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tadministrative = append(administrative, p)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn administrativeRecordsAreRegular(root, administrative)\n}\n\n// materialTrackedUnchanged reports whether no tracked file differs from HEAD\n// outside the administrative records. Untracked files are not counted: checks\n// leave build products behind, and those are not the material they ran over.\nfunc materialTrackedUnchanged(root string) bool {\n\tout, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 16*1024*1024, \"status\", \"--porcelain=v1\", \"-z\", \"--ignore-submodules=none\", \"--untracked-files=no\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\trecords := strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\")\n\tvar administrative []string\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(records); i++ {\n\t\trecord := records[i]\n\t\tif len(record) < 4 {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpaths := []string{record[3:]}\n\t\tif record[0] == 'R' || record[0] == 'C' || record[1] == 'R' || record[1] == 'C' {\n\t\t\tif i+1 < len(records) {\n\t\t\t\ti++\n\t\t\t\tpaths = append(paths, records[i])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\t\tif !posemodel.IsAdministrativePath(p) {\n\t\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tadministrative = append(administrative, p)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn administrativeRecordsAreRegular(root, administrative)\n}\n\n// administrativeRecordsAreRegular reports whether every changed administrative\n// path is a regular file at HEAD (or new): editing a committed symlink or\n// submodule inside a class is a material change, as in MaterialSetDigest.\nfunc administrativeRecordsAreRegular(root string, paths []string) bool {\n\tif len(paths) == 0 {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\targs := []string{\"ls-tree\", \"-z\", \"HEAD\", \"--\"}\n\tfor _, p := range paths {\n\t\tif strings.ContainsAny(p, \"\\n\\x00\") {\n\t\t\treturn false\n\t\t}\n\t\t// The working copy as well as HEAD: a regular record replaced by a link, or\nfunc discoverValidationModulesIn(files posemodel.Files, root string) ([]validationModule, error) {\n\t// testdata/fixture(s): see index.go's scanModules for why these are\n\t// excluded (spec pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes).\n\tignored := map[string]bool{\".git\": true, \".qwen\": true, \"node_modules\": true, \"vendor\": true, \".venv\": true, \".pnpm-store\": true, \"target\": true, \"dist\": true, \"build\": true, \".next\": true, \"coverage\": true, \".pose\": true, \"testdata\": true, \"fixture\": true, \"fixtures\": true}\n\t// A gitignored subtree (a locally-checked-out project the repository\n\t// deliberately excludes, e.g. a vendored tool) is never a governed\n\t// deliverable module — see posemodel.GitIgnoredPaths (spec\n\t// pose-discovery-gitignore-and-root-alias-fix).\n\tgitIgnored := posemodel.GitIgnoredPathsIn(files, root)\n\tbyPath := map[string]string{}\n\terr := posemodel.WalkDirIn(files, root, func(path string, entry os.DirEntry, err error) error {\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif entry.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tif path != root && ignored[entry.Name()] {\n\t\t\t\treturn filepath.SkipDir\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif path != root {\n\t\t\t\tif rel, relErr := filepath.Rel(root, path); relErr == nil && (gitIgnored[filepath.ToSlash(rel)+\"/\"] || gitIgnored[filepath.ToSlash(rel)]) {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn filepath.SkipDir\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\t// spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion: the Python manager (poetry/\n\t\t// pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517) is resolved by the matrix `when`\n\t\t// predicates, not at discovery time — every marker maps to the\n\t\t// \"python\" stack. stackForManifestFile is shared with scanModules\n\t\t// (spec pose-validation-scanner-consolidation) so a stack-detection\n\t\t// fix here applies there too.\n\t\tstack := stackForManifestFile(entry.Name())\n\t\tif stack != \"\" {\n\t\t\t// Existing overwrite-by-walk-order semantics preserved\n\t\t\t// unchanged for compatibility; `pose stacks` surfaces the full\n\t\t\t// per-directory catalog resolution (including any conflicting\n\t\t\t// markers) independently of what this single-stack map picks.\n\t\t\tbyPath[filepath.Dir(path)] = stack\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn nil\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tmodules := make([]validationModule, 0, len(byPath))\n\tfor abs, stack := range byPath {\n\t\tif stack == \"java\" && isAndroidModuleIn(files, abs) {\n\t\t\t// A Gradle module carrying an AndroidManifest.xml is an Android\n\t\t\t// app/library, not a generic JVM backend module — conflating the\n\t\t\t// two under \"java\" made every Android module's domain\n\t\t\t// misleading (spec pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes). Kotlin-only\n\t\t\t// non-Android Gradle modules are unaffected: they have no\n\t\t\t// AndroidManifest.xml and keep classifying as \"java\".\n\t\t\tstack = \"android\"\n\t\t}\n\t\trel, err := filepath.Rel(root, abs)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn nil, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tmodules = append(modules, validationModule{filepath.ToSlash(rel), abs, stack})\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(modules, func(i, j int) bool { return modules[i].Rel < modules[j].Rel })\n\treturn modules, nil\n}\n\n// discoverValidationModules reads and writes the filesystem directly; discoverValidationModulesIn takes the\n// operation's files.\nfunc discoverValidationModules(root string) ([]validationModule, error) {\n\treturn discoverValidationModulesIn(posemodel.NativeFiles(), root)\n}\n\n\t\t}\n\t\tabs := filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(clean))\n\t\tif info, err := os.Stat(abs); err == nil && info.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tstack := override.Stack\n\t\t\tif stack == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tstack = \"contract\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tmodules = append(modules, validationModule{Rel: clean, Abs: abs, Stack: stack})\n\t\t\tknownModules[clean] = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tsort.Slice(modules, func(i, j int) bool { return modules[i].Rel < modules[j].Rel })\n\tvar scope *scopeSelection\n\tif changedTo != \"\" && changedFrom == \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintln(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: --changed-to requires --changed-from.\", \"Erro: --changed-to exige --changed-from.\"))\n\t\treturn 2\n\t}\n\tif changedFrom != \"\" {\n\t\tsel, err := computeChangedScope(root, changedFrom, changedTo, modules)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stderr, cliText(locale, \"Error: changed-scope selection: %v\\n\", \"Erro: seleção por escopo alterado: %v\\n\"), err)\n\t\t\treturn 2\n\t\t}\n\t\tscope = &sel\n\t\tif explain {\n\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"[changed-scope] %s: %d changed file(s), %d/%d module(s) selected\\n\", sel.rangeLabel(), len(sel.Changed), len(sel.Selected), len(modules))\n\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"  + %s: %s\\n\", m.Rel, reason)\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintf(stdout, \"  - %s: not affected by %s\\n\", m.Rel, sel.rangeLabel())\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\trun := validationRunResult{\n\t\tSchemaVersion: validationResultSchema,\n\t\tGeneratedAt:   time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339),\n\t\tMode:          mode,\n\t\tStackFilter:   stackFilter,\n\t\tModuleFilter:  moduleFilter,\n\t\tChecks:        []checkResult{},\n\t}\n\tmatrixDigest := sha256.Sum256(raw)\n\trun.MatrixSHA256 = \"sha256:\" + hex.EncodeToString(matrixDigest[:])\n\trun.EngineVersion = validationEngineIdentity()\n---\ntype: decision-log\nslug: module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance\nowner: @pose-maintainers\nsensitivity: public-internal\ncreated_at: 2026-08-16\nlast_reviewed_at: 2026-08-16\nexpires_at: 2026-11-14\nsource_refs:\n  spec: \"pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes\"\n  workflow: \"bugfix\"\n  commands: [\"pose update --force\", \"pose install\", \"pose index\", \"pose review bundle --seal\"]\n  external_sources: []\n---\n\n# decision-log: module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance\n\n## Context\n\nAuditing `pose update`/`pose install` across 7 real repositories ahead of\nthe v1.4.0 release found that running either command on `pose-dist` itself\n(the product's own dogfooding instance) discovered\n`examples/brownfield-kits/direct-adoption/fixture/service/go.mod` — an\nillustrative fixture, not a real deliverable module — as a genuine module\nand added it to `.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json`. That single addition\nrecomputed the global provenance digest (`graph.ProvenanceDigest`, computed\nover the whole delivery-integrity graph, not per-spec — see the\n`pose-gate-closeout-procedure` session note this decision-log\ngeneralizes) and superseded the review attestation of every already-closed\nspec whose scope touches `module-metadata.json`: between 3 and 22 specs\nacross the audit's trial runs, depending on which other specs had most\nrecently closed.\n\n## Current state\n\nFixed the trigger only (spec `pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes`, R7):\n`testdata`, `fixture` and `fixtures` directories are now excluded from\nmodule discovery in all three independent walkers\n(`discoverValidationModules` in validate.go, `scanModules` in index.go,\nand `internal/pose/discovery.go`'s capability scanner). A genuine,\nintentional module-metadata change — a real module added, removed or\nreclassified — still invalidates every closed spec's review attestation\nthat touches it, exactly as designed today. That broader behavior was\ndeliberately left unchanged (Decision 2 in the spec above): narrowing the\nprovenance digest so it only invalidates reviews whose scope actually\nincludes the changed module is a real design trade-off (what counts as\n\"unrelated,\" whether a narrower digest weakens the guarantee it exists to\nprovide) that deserves its own spec and explicit review, not a side effect\nof fixing a discovery false-positive.\n\n## Next checks\n\n- None deterministic yet. If a future spec pursues narrowing the\n  provenance digest, `pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go`\n  (`ProvenanceDigest`) and `pose review bundle --seal`'s scope-provenance\n  matching are the two places to start reading.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Any *other* directory-discovery path added in the future (a fourth\n  scanner, or a stack detector that walks the tree independently) needs\n  the same `testdata`/`fixture`/`fixtures` exclusion applied by hand —\n  there is no single shared walker all three call today (each has its own\n  `ignored` map/condition; see the follow-up already on record about this\n  in `pose-review-bundle-roadmap-path-portability`/session knowledge about\n  \"enumerate by hand\" gaps recurring across this repository).\n- Every maintainer who runs `pose update --force`/`pose install` on\n  `pose-dist` itself after a *real* module-metadata change should expect\n  to reseal/reattest every closed spec whose review scope includes\n  `module-metadata.json`, per `pose-gate-closeout-procedure` — this is\n  expected, not a bug, and this decision-log's fix does not change it.\n\n## Next owner\n\n@pose-maintainers — no active handoff; revisit only if the digest-scoping\nfollow-up (spec `pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes`, Follow-ups) is picked up.\n\n## References\n\n- spec:pose-upgrade-path-audit-fixes (Decision 2, R7, Non-goals)\n- knowledge: session note `pose-gate-closeout-procedure` (this repository's\n  own local agent memory, not a `.pose/knowledge/` artifact) — first\n  documented that the provenance digest is global-over-the-graph, not\n  per-spec, and that reseal/reattest is required after any reindex that\n  changes it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
