{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'func cmdArtifactCheck' pose-mcp/internal/cli; rg --files pose-mcp/internal/pose | rg artifact; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go | sed -n '1417,1448p'; rg -n 'same actor|actor|revision|head' .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md\"","aggregated_output":"pose-mcp/internal/cli/artifact_integrity.go:787:func cmdArtifactCheck(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/artifact_claims_boundary_test.go\npose-mcp/internal/pose/artifacts.go\npose-mcp/internal/pose/artifact_ref_test.go\npose-mcp/internal/pose/artifact_attribution_index_test.go\npose-mcp/internal/pose/artifact_ref.go\npose-mcp/internal/pose/artifacts_test.go\n  1417\t\t\tif !verbose && (result.Outcome == \"fail\" || result.Outcome == \"error\") {\n  1418\t\t\t\tstep.Detail(tailLines(result.Output, 10), jsonOut)\n  1419\t\t\t}\n  1420\t\t\trun.Checks = append(run.Checks, *result)\n  1421\t\t}\n  1422\t\trun.WallSeconds = time.Since(wallStart).Seconds()\n  1423\t\t// A check that changed a tracked file changed the material it ran over: the\n  1424\t\t// committed tree no longer describes what was validated.\n  1425\t\tendedClean := materialTrackedUnchanged(root)\n  1426\t\t// The run validated its recorded head only if HEAD never moved: a commit\n  1427\t\t// made while checks ran would let them observe code the record does not\n  1428\t\t// name (review round 4, N10).\n  1429\t\tendHead := \"\"\n  1430\t\tif head, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 1024, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"); err == nil {\n  1431\t\t\tendHead = strings.TrimSpace(string(head))\n  1432\t\t}\n  1433\t\trun.MaterialClean = startedClean && endedClean && run.GitHead != \"\" && endHead == run.GitHead\n  1434\t\t// A run that did not validate its recorded head binds no material: its\n  1435\t\t// bindings would let a later tree make a misattributed pass current again\n  1436\t\t// (review round 5, N12).\n  1437\t\tif len(run.MaterialProvenance) > 0 && !run.MaterialClean {\n  1438\t\t\tfor spec := range run.MaterialProvenance {\n  1439\t\t\t\trun.MaterialProvenance[spec] = posemodel.MaterialBindingUnavailable\n  1440\t\t\t}\n  1441\t\t}\n  1442\t\t// A cancelled run never reads as a success, whatever the severity of the\n  1443\t\t// checks it stopped (review round 1, B5).\n  1444\t\tcancelled := false\n  1445\t\tfor _, check := range run.Checks {\n  1446\t\t\tcancelled = cancelled || check.LimitState == \"cancelled\"\n  1447\t\t}\n  1448\t\tfor _, line := range validationTimingNotes(run) {\n15:- a head that is an ancestor of the current head;\n19:With a usable base, the run executes the changed-scope selection between the two heads. That selection ignores the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings already ignore: the engine's closed list, still regular files at `HEAD`. It copies the base results of every unselected module. Each copied result keeps its outcome and states the head where it really ran (`carried_from`) and when (`carried_generated_at`). The delivery reading uses that head for the result. Any unmet condition runs everything and records the reason.\n25:A change confined to one module reruns that module, its declared dependents and the modules that cannot claim self-containment. Everything else is reused with its true origin visible. Consumers that compare a result's head with a subject head now see carried results as observations of the earlier head, which is accurate. An incorrect `selfContained` declaration can carry a stale result. That risk sits with the instance's explicit declaration and is documented next to it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"agent_message","text":"I found a second alias gap on case-insensitive filesystems: dependency paths are compared as text, and the physical-directory alias check covers only module directories. A dependency spelled `A/input` can therefore miss a change recorded by Git as `a/input`.\n\nThe broader sweep also covered nested ownership, manifest-free overrides, stack changes, policy widening, chained origins, and atomic canonical publication. I’m separating confirmed defects from assumptions and test limits in the draft."}}
